 _SLIDING_NEWTON_TOL = wp.constant(1.0e-7)
 """Tolerance for the Newton method to solve for the sliding velocity"""
 
-_INCLUDE_LEFTOVER_STRAIN = wp.constant(False)
+_INCLUDE_LEFTOVER_STRAIN = wp.constant(True)
 """Whether to include leftover strain (due to not fully-converged implicit solve) in the elastic strain.
 
-More accurate, but less stable for stiff materials. Development toggle for experimentation."""
+MUST be True for the scheme to be consistent. The kinematic identity
+dt * B v = d_eps_e + d_eps_p holds exactly, by definition of the elastic/plastic
+decomposition, independently of how well the implicit system was solved. With
+this flag False the difference between the plastic strain the solve converged to
+and the one the flow rule actually wants -- i.e. the constitutive residual of the
+final iterate -- is excluded from the elastic strain and silently absorbed into
+the plastic strain, which is irreversible. The material then has no memory of the
+compression it underwent, so it offers no extra resistance on the next step and
+an under-converged step becomes a permanent position error instead of a bounded,
+self-correcting stress error. That is a one-way ratchet: a body resting on the
+material creeps at a rate set by the solver residual rather than by the
+constitutive law, and never recovers.
+
+With this flag True the leftover enters eps_e and may be inadmissible (outside
+the yield set K). That is correct and is handled on the next step as a standard
+elastic predictor followed by the return map (solve_flow_rule / project_stress).
+The original objection -- "less stable for stiff materials" -- is a symptom of
+the same defect: a material stiff enough that the leftover is large is a material
+whose elastic branch the solve is failing to resolve."""
 
 _USE_CAM_CLAY = wp.constant(False)
 """Use Modified Cam-Clay flow rule instead of the piecewise-linear anisotropic one. Development toggle for experimentation."""
